  "preamble": "Monophthongization of the diphthong /ei/ to a mid front monophthong /e/ (often long [eː]) through absorption of the off-glide. It is a recurrent change: Attic Greek ει became [eː] before the Classical period, East Norse turned æi into ē (stæin > sten 'stone') from the 10th century, and many European Portuguese vernaculars reduce ei to [e] (queijo [ˈkeʒu]). Georgian lost the glide of Old Georgian final ey, written ჱ or ეჲ (მზჱ mzey > მზე mze 'sun').",
  "phoneticEffects": "Monophthongization, Loss of Off-glide",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Ancient Greek (Attic)",
      "languageFamily": "Hellenic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "εἶμι /éi.mi/ 'I go'",
          "to": "[êː.mi]",
          "note": "The diphthongs /ei ou/ became the long monophthongs /eː oː/ before the Classical period; /ei/ remained only before vowels."
        },
        {
          "from": "λείπω 'I leave' with the diphthong ει",
          "to": "[lěː.pɔː] (Classical Attic)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Old East Norse (Old Danish, Old Swedish)",
      "languageFamily": "North Germanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "runic stæin 'stone' (Old West Norse steinn)",
          "to": "sten",
          "note": "The monophthongization æi > ē began in mid-10th-century Denmark and is the chief feature separating Old East Norse from Old West Norse; Old Gutnish kept stain."
        },
        {
          "from": "Old Norse bein 'bone'",
          "to": "Old Swedish, Old Danish ben (Swedish ben /ˈbeːn/)"
        },
        {
          "from": "Old Norse heill 'whole'",
          "to": "Old Swedish hēl, Old Danish hel (Swedish hel /heːl/)"
        },
        {
          "from": "Old Norse geit 'goat'",
          "to": "Old Swedish gēt, Old Danish get"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "European Portuguese (vernacular)",
      "languageFamily": "Ibero-Romance",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "queijo /ˈkejʒu/ 'cheese'",
          "to": "[ˈkeʒu]",
          "note": "Optional monophthongization of ei before a palatal and before consonants, depending on speaker and region; Lisbon instead has [ɐj]."
